# GaggiMate firmware tests

This directory holds the firmware's unit tests, run with
[PlatformIO's bundled Unity framework](https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html).

There are two ways tests run:

- **Host (`native`) tests** — pure firmware *logic* compiled and run on the dev
  machine with a normal C++ compiler. No ESP32, no flashing, no board. Fast and
  what you run during development and (eventually) in CI.
- **On-target tests** — the same Unity tests can also be built for a real board
  (e.g. `pio test -e display`). The test files use `#ifdef ARDUINO` to pick
  `setup()`/`loop()` on-target vs `main()` on host.

## Running the host tests

```sh
pio test -e native
```

This builds every `test/test_*/` suite against the host and runs them, printing
a per-test pass/fail summary. Exit code is non-zero if any assertion fails.

> The `native` platform uses your machine's C/C++ toolchain (gcc/clang on
> Linux/macOS, MinGW/Clang on Windows). If `pio test -e native` reports a
> missing compiler, install one (e.g. `apt install build-essential`,
> `xcode-select --install`, or a MinGW-w64 build on Windows) and retry.

## Directory convention

Each suite lives in its own folder named `test_<thing>/` containing a single
`*.cpp` with its Unity test functions and a runner:

```
test/
  test_event_system/test_event_system.cpp          # plugin/event system
  test_profile_validation/test_parse_profile.cpp    # profile JSON parsing
  test_ble_scale_scan_policy/test_ble_scale_scan_policy.cpp
  native/                                            # host-only build support
```

PlatformIO discovers each `test_*` folder automatically. Inside a suite, the
runner is structured so the same file works on host and on-target. Each suite
also defines empty `setUp()`/`tearDown()` functions, which Unity requires:

```cpp
void setUp(void) {}
void tearDown(void) {}

static int runMyTests() {
    UNITY_BEGIN();
    RUN_TEST(test_something);
    return UNITY_END();
}

#ifdef ARDUINO
void setup() { runMyTests(); }
void loop() {}
#else
int main(int, char **) { return runMyTests(); }
#endif
```

## How the hardware seams work (host builds)

Firmware logic headers `#include <Arduino.h>` for the `String` type and pull in
`ESP_LOG*` macros, neither of which exist off-target. To compile that logic on
the host without dragging in FreeRTOS / NimBLE / the ESP-IDF, the `native`
environment in `platformio.ini` adds `test/native/` to the front of the include
path. That directory provides the seams:

- **`test/native/Arduino.h`** — a tiny shim: a `std::string`-backed `String`
  with the subset of the Arduino `WString` API the tested code uses, plus no-op
  `ESP_LOG*` macros. Because it is first on the include path, `#include
  <Arduino.h>` resolves here instead of the real platform header.
- **`test/native/*.cpp`** — host re-implementations of pure, hardware-free free
  functions that the logic links against but whose production definitions live
  in translation units that can't compile off-target (today: `isSafeId()`, whose
  production source is `src/display/core/utils.cpp`). Keep these in sync with the
  production logic.

`ArduinoJson` is header-only and compiles natively as-is, so profile parsing is
tested against the real parser. `src/display/core/PluginManager.cpp` is compiled
straight into the host build (see `build_src_filter` in `[env:native]`) because
its only platform dependency is `String`, satisfied by the shim.

### Adding a new host test

1. Create `test/test_<thing>/test_<thing>.cpp` with the runner pattern above
   (including the empty `setUp`/`tearDown`).
2. If the code under test needs production `.cpp` files, add them to
   `build_src_filter` in `[env:native]`. Prefer testing logic that lives in
   headers or that has no hardware dependencies.
3. If it needs a hardware-free free function whose definition can't build on
   host, add a host implementation under `test/native/` (and keep it in sync).
4. Resist growing `test/native/Arduino.h`. If a test needs a lot more of the
   Arduino surface, that's a signal to seam the production code behind a thin
   interface / test double instead.

> **CI:** wiring `pio test -e native` into CI is tracked separately in CAR-340.
